# Biome 2, level 2 — The Bramble
#
# A thin path across a wide place. Where The Long Valley sprawls at fifty-five cells, this is
# twenty-eight over a footprint just as large: two zigzags of bends at opposite corners, a straight run
# between them, and a handful of spurs that go nowhere. Panning here is travel rather than survey.
#
# Every cell of a zigzag needs a bend, because a pointy-top hexagon has no northern neighbour and
# "down" therefore alternates south-east and south-west. That is the lesson biome one teaches on its
# second level, at three cells; this asks for it six times in a row, twice, across a board that cannot
# be seen at once.
#
# The spurs matter more than they look. A board of nothing but forced cells is a transcription
# exercise — biome one's own second level says as much — so each route carries somewhere wrong to go,
# and the wind run's branch at 0,2 will take a bend legally and lead nowhere.
#
# Edge directions run clockwise on screen from due east: 0 east, 1 south-east, 2 south-west, 3 west,
# 4 north-west, 5 north-east.

id: biome2-02
name: The Bramble

base-score: 100

# Either zigzag plus the wind run clears it; a zigzag alone does not. Six conduits pay 448, four pay
# 246, so the cheapest way through is 694 against this, and all three pairs reach 1,142.
target-score: 650

tokens: 1
skips: 3

seed: 19

cell: 0,-8
cell: 0,-7
cell: -1,-6
cell: -1,-5
cell: -2,-4
cell: -2,-3
cell: -3,-2
cell: -3,-1
cell: 6,-2
cell: 6,-1
cell: 5,0
cell: 5,1
cell: 4,2
cell: 4,3
cell: 3,4
cell: 3,5
cell: -2,1
cell: -1,1
cell: 0,1
cell: 1,1
cell: 2,1
cell: 3,1
cell: 1,-7
cell: -2,-6
cell: 7,-2
cell: 2,4
cell: 0,2
cell: 1,2

spring: 0,-8 water
hub: -3,-1 water
spring: 6,-2 crystal
hub: 3,5 crystal
spring: -2,1 wind
hub: 3,1 wind

# Bends for the two zigzags, straights for the run between them. One tile of the wrong shape in each of
# two kinds, because a bag that can only be placed correctly makes the skip control decoration.
tile: 0,2 water x7
tile: 0,3 water x1
tile: 0,2 crystal x7
tile: 0,3 wind x5
tile: 0,2 wind x1

# The board's own solution, replayed by the solvability gate rather than searched for. The search
# exhausted six hundred thousand states here in sixteen seconds without a verdict — open space is what
# makes it explode, and a thin path across a wide place is mostly open space.
#
# It clears the board on the two zigzags alone, at 896 against a target of 650, and leaves the wind run
# three conduits short. That is deliberate: this solution is the cheapest way through rather than the
# board's ceiling, because the cheapest way is the one worth proving reachable.
#
# Two of the three skips are spent, on tiles that arrive before there is anywhere legal to put them.
# Rotations are written down because the zigzags alternate between two of them and a spur will accept
# the wrong one legally.

solve: -1,1 rot 0
solve: 0,-7 rot 2
solve: -1,-6 rot 5
solve: skip
solve: 0,1 rot 0
solve: -1,-5 rot 2
solve: -2,-4 rot 5
solve: 6,-1 rot 2
solve: 1,1 rot 0
solve: 5,0 rot 5
solve: 5,1 rot 2
solve: skip
solve: -2,-3 rot 2
solve: -3,-2 rot 5
solve: 4,2 rot 5
solve: 4,3 rot 2
solve: 3,4 rot 5
